 near the top of the Democratic tier in the upcoming presidential race. The state's fifteen-point Democratic partisan lean, combined with an open contest after the term-limited Republican incumbent president, gives the Democratic nominee a clear structural advantage. No candidate-level polling or expert ratings are available, so the forecast rests on fundamentals.\n\nRecent news underscores the state's Democratic orientation: Governor Wes Moore is running for re-election amid national speculation about his future, and Democratic lawmakers are pursuing an aggressive redistricting plan that Republicans are challenging in court. Neither development changes the presidential picture materially. The expected margin is about fifteen points Democratic, with uncertainty wide enough that a strong Republican national environment could narrow it, though a Republican win would require a shift well beyond anything in the current evidence.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9506, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9102, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9258, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9194, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9136, "margin": 15.0}]}